{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1585589040/2/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"446ef5d2\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.\n- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.\n\nRule:\n- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\n- Translate pieces (no rotation) to tile the rectangle; touching edges must match, with rect_col as wildcard.\n\nKey insight: rect_col acts as wildcard during edge matching — only non-rect_col pixels at shared boundaries must agree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"409aa875\" lines=\"68-81\">\n<description>\nFire projectiles from 3-pixel triangle sprites; mark collisions with maroon or blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg 3-pixel sprites shaped as axis-aligned L's or diagonal V's.\n\nRule:\n- Each triangle has an apex and an emission direction determined by its shape: L-shapes fire diagonally away from the missing corner; V-shapes fire along the axis away from the open side.\n- A projectile lands exactly 5 cells from the apex in the emission direction.\n- If it hits an existing sprite, that entire sprite is recolored to maroon (9).\n- If two projectiles land on the same empty cell, that cell becomes blue (1); a lone projectile on empty bg paints maroon (9).\n\nKey insight: The triangle's orientation encodes both direction and target; dual-projectile collisions on open bg produce blue, not maroon.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\" lines=\"6-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\" lines=\"275-288\">\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5dbc8537\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nFill rectangular cavities in a template shape by placing source sprites into matching holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. A dominant \"base\" color forms a large frame/template with rectangular gaps.\n- Small colored sprites sit outside the template region.\n\nRule:\n- The base color (largest connected component excluding bg) defines the template bbox.\n- Each rectangular hole inside the template matches exactly one outside sprite by size.\n- Place each sprite into its matching cavity; output is the filled template bbox.\n\nKey insight: Sprite bounding boxes match cavity dimensions exactly — it's a jigsaw-fit puzzle where each piece has a unique slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e3721c99\" lines=\"69-83\">\n<description>\nRecolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; grey (5) sprites scattered across the grid.\n- Colored legend shapes (non-bg, non-grey, at least 2×2, not touching the outer border) near grid edge,\n  each with a distinct number of enclosed holes.\n\nRule:\n- Each colored legend shape defines a mapping: hole count → color.\n- For every grey sprite, count its holes (enclosed bg regions not touching the sprite's bounding-box border).\n- Recolor the grey sprite to the matching legend color; erase grey distractors with unlisted hole counts.\n\nKey insight: Shape identity is encoded by topology (number of holes), not geometry or size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"64efde09\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nCopy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg is the most common color.\n- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.\n- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.\n- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
