lide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/tem3/combo_prompt_88e364bc_42.txt:200:\"\"\"Generator for task 88e364bc (slide yellow in walled regions by legend arrows).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:26:#   1. one green arrow glyph placed near the target anchor,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:28:# - The arrow gives the mandatory \"primary\" reflection:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:29:#     right arrow  -> LR reflection is required,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:30:#     down arrow   -> UD reflection is required.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:32:#   also needed. Concretely, after reading the arrow, the solver compares the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:38:#     1. read the local arrow; if it is a down arrow, start from UD instead,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:42:#   Difference: quadrant position no longer selects the mirror; the arrow gives\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:46:# - The arrow vocabulary stays tiny: exactly two 3x3 glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:49:#   arrows alone are insufficient, and anchors alone are ambiguous without first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:50:#   knowing which primary axis the arrow selected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:60:#   must remove arrow markers before stamping or they would leak into the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:62:#   arrows exist only outside TL, and the TL motif remains one 4-connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:66:# - The right arrow is drawn 1 cell above the anchor; the down arrow is drawn 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:71:# - The arrow is kept disconnected from the anchor, so component extraction for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:75:# - A human can read the arrow as the obvious first instruction, then notice\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:76:#   that two targets with the same arrow can still differ because of the anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:78:# - The generator enforces both arrow types, both anchor states, and at least\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:82:# - Green is reserved for arrows and never used inside the motif.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:84:#   anchor, while arrows sit outside with a one-cell gap. This avoids overlap and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:88:# - No hidden color ordering is used. Red still marks anchors, green means arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:91:#   \"arrows only\" or \"original quadrant rule\" at the whole-output level.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:95:# - Every non-TL quadrant has exactly one arrow glyph and exactly one decorated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:97:# - Each target anchor matches exactly one of two templates once its arrow-chosen\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:212:def _read_arrow_primaries(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:225:        assert key != (0, 0), \"arrows are only allowed in non-TL quadrants\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:226:        assert key not in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:229:            raise AssertionError(\"unrecognized arrow glyph\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:233:        assert key in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:254:    Replicate the TL motif across quadrants with size scaling, arrow-selected primary reflection, and anchor-selected extra reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:259:    - Each non-TL quadrant contains one decorated red anchor plus exactly one nearby green arrow glyph; the anchor size still varies by quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:263:    - In each target quadrant, read the arrow first to get the mandatory reflection axis: right arrow means LR, down arrow means UD.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:264:    - Compare the observed target-anchor template against the TL anchor under either that primary flip alone or both flips; apply the matching transform to the full source motif, scale by anchor-size ratio, align the red region to the target anchor, and stamp non-background pixels. Remove the green arrows from the final output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:266:    Key insight: The arrow does not fully specify the transform by itself. It only chooses the primary axis; the anchor's internal asymmetry tells you whether an additional orthogonal flip must be composed on top of it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:275:    primaries, marker_components = _read_arrow_primaries(g, mid_r, mid_c)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:515:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:520:def _place_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, anchor_r: int, anchor_c: int, primary: tuple[bool, bool]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:523:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r - 4, anchor_c, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:525:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r, anchor_c - 4, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:611:    arrow_only_out = _build_output(patch, anchor_patch, tl_r0, tl_c0, target_sizes, anchors, primaries)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:613:    if int(np.count_nonzero(out != arrow_only_out)) < MIN_VARIANT_DIFF:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:624:        _place_arrow(inp, ar, ac, primaries[key])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:627:    # it is the reason arrow_only_out differs from out.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:970:    At a marked junction, the color+diagonal arrow selects a corner pair and overrides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:1047:    Send each token through the cyan network, but read colored arrow junctions before deciding where to turn.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:1051:    - The wire system uses color 8 for ordinary pipe, color 9 for pass-through blocks, and colors 4/5 for turn-marker junction cells plus their diagonal arrow decoration.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:1060:    - Continuous channel columns make many junctions ambiguous, so the correct route comes from decoding the local arrow markers, not from a generic straight-ahead heuristic.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:1320:    at those cells the tracer can keep going straight unless it reads the local arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:3355:  • Measure fusion-specific behavior to verify non-degenerate red/blue priority over default arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/logs/combo_a47bf94d/20260503_054255/gen_2.log:3427:+#    - solver 假设每个箭头中心都带有同家族的单个对角装饰，generator 的 _place_arrow_marker() 保证这一点。\n/h